mod('enzymes','Enzymes & activation energy',
 'Reactions in cells would be far too slow at body temperature without help. Enzymes make them fast enough to sustain life, and do it one reaction at a time.',
 ['An <b>enzyme</b> is a protein catalyst. It binds its <b>substrate</b> at the <b>active site</b>, whose shape and chemistry fit that substrate (the active site adjusts slightly on binding, <b>induced fit</b>). Enzymes speed reactions by <b>lowering the activation energy</b> (the energy hurdle to get started). They do not change the free energy of the reactants or products, do not make an unfavorable reaction favorable, and are not used up.',
  'Reaction rate rises with substrate concentration until all active sites are busy (saturation), then levels off at V<sub>max</sub>. A <b>competitive inhibitor</b> resembles the substrate and blocks the active site; more substrate can outcompete it, so V<sub>max</sub> can still be reached. A <b>noncompetitive (allosteric) inhibitor</b> binds elsewhere and changes the shape of the enzyme, lowering the V<sub>max</sub> that can be reached.'],
 ('chemical reactions needed high temperatures or harsh conditions','that a protein can lower the energy barrier while leaving the overall energy change alone','why reactions in cells are fast and specific','activation energy, saturation and inhibition'),
 [('v = Vmax[S] / (Km + [S])','shape of the rate curve (model)'),('catalyst lowers E<sub>a</sub>','ΔG unchanged'),('competitive: raises apparent Km','Vmax reachable with more substrate'),('noncompetitive: lowers Vmax','more substrate does not fix it')],
 [lab('en-lab','Rate against substrate concentration',canvas='en-canvas',aria='Graph of reaction rate against substrate concentration, with the chosen inhibitor and the uninhibited curve for comparison.',
   seg=('en-inh','Inhibitor',[('none','none'),('comp','competitive'),('nonc','noncompetitive')]),
   ctl=[('en-s','Substrate concentration',0,100,1,20),('en-e','Amount of enzyme',1,10,1,5)],
   out=[('en-v','rate','x'),('en-pct','percent of maximum','a'),('en-lim','what limits the rate now','v')],own=True),
  lab('en-lab2','The energy hill',canvas='en-canvas2',aria='Energy profile of a reaction with and without an enzyme. The enzyme lowers the peak but not the start and end levels.',
   seg=('en-rx','Reaction',[('exo','releases energy (exergonic)'),('endo','needs energy (endergonic)')]),
   pre='<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn primary" id="en-cat">Add enzyme</button></div>',
   out=[('en-ea','activation energy','x'),('en-dg','ΔG (products − reactants)','a'),('en-cm','effect','')],own=True)],
 [('Choose competitive inhibition, then raise substrate to 100. What happens to the rate? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#en-inh [data-k=comp]"],["#en-s",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','It climbs back almost to the uninhibited maximum: lots of substrate outcompetes the inhibitor.'),
  ('Choose noncompetitive inhibition and raise substrate to 100. Does it recover? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#en-inh [data-k=nonc]"],["#en-s",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','No. The ceiling is lower because some enzyme molecules are altered whatever the substrate level.'),
  ('Add the enzyme to the energy profile. What changes and what does not? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#en-cat"]) + '\'>Add enzyme</button>','','The peak (activation energy) falls. The start and end levels, and so ΔG, stay the same.')],
 ('A reaction has an activation energy of 80 kJ/mol and ΔG = −20 kJ/mol. An enzyme lowers the activation energy to 45 kJ/mol. State the new activation energy and ΔG, and the effect on rate.',[
  '<b>Activation energy.</b> 45 kJ/mol (lowered by 35).',
  '<b>ΔG.</b> Still −20 kJ/mol. An enzyme cannot change the energy of reactants or products.',
  '<b>Rate.</b> Faster, because a larger fraction of molecules has enough energy to react. Equilibrium is reached sooner but is not moved.']),
 ['Saying enzymes add energy to the reaction.','Saying enzymes make a nonspontaneous reaction spontaneous.','Believing enzymes are used up.','Mixing competitive and noncompetitive inhibitors.','Forgetting that rate levels off because enzyme is limiting, not substrate.'],
 ('Reading rate graphs',['On a rate vs. substrate graph, a flat region means the enzyme is saturated: adding substrate does nothing, adding enzyme would. A rising region means substrate is limiting. If the inhibited curve reaches the same maximum, the inhibitor is competitive; if the maximum is lower, it is noncompetitive.']),
 [('An enzyme lowers the activation energy of a reaction. Which quantity is unchanged?',['the free energy change (ΔG)','the activation energy','the reaction rate','the energy of the transition state'],0,'ΔG depends only on reactants and products, not on the path.')])

mod('enzyenv','Temperature, pH & enzyme activity',
 'Enzymes work best in a narrow range. Move out of it and the protein changes shape and the reaction slows or stops.',
 ['Each enzyme has an <b>optimum temperature</b> and <b>optimum pH</b> fitted to the place it works: pepsin works at about pH 2 in the stomach, trypsin at about pH 8 in the small intestine, and the polymerase from hot-spring bacteria works near 72 °C. Rates rise with temperature because molecules collide more often and with more energy; past the optimum the enzyme <b>denatures</b> and the rate falls steeply.',
  'pH changes the charges on amino acid R groups, which changes ionic and hydrogen bonds and so the active site. Extreme pH can denature the enzyme. These effects show up on exam graphs as bell-shaped curves; the peak marks the optimum. A graph with temperature on the x-axis that rises, peaks and then drops quickly is the typical pattern.'],
 ('rates just increased with temperature','that enzymes are proteins whose shape depends on temperature and pH','why enzymes in different organs have different optima','optimum conditions and denaturation'),
 [('rate rises with temperature up to the optimum','then falls from denaturation'),('optimum pH differs by enzyme','pepsin ≈ 2, trypsin ≈ 8'),('denaturation: shape lost, active site no longer fits','usually irreversible when severe'),('Q<sub>10</sub> ≈ 2 below the optimum','rate doubles per 10 °C')],
 [lab('ee-lab','Find the optimum',canvas='ee-canvas',aria='Two graphs of enzyme activity: one against temperature and one against pH, with markers at the chosen values.',
   seg=('ee-en','Enzyme',[('pep','pepsin (stomach)'),('try','trypsin (small intestine)'),('taq','Taq polymerase (hot springs)')]),
   ctl=[('ee-t','Temperature',0,100,1,37),('ee-ph','pH',1,13,.1,7)],
   out=[('ee-ft','activity from temperature','x'),('ee-fp','activity from pH','a'),('ee-all','overall activity','v'),('ee-st','what is happening','')],own=True)],
 [('Choose pepsin and set pH to 2 at 37 °C. Then raise the pH to 8. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#ee-en [data-k=pep]"],["#ee-t",37],["#ee-ph",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Activity is near the maximum at pH 2 and almost zero at pH 8: pepsin is adapted to stomach acid.'),
  ('Choose Taq and run it at 37 °C and at 72 °C. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#ee-en [data-k=taq]"],["#ee-t",72],["#ee-ph",8.3]) + '\'>72 °C</button>','','At 37 °C it is slow (cold for this enzyme); at 72 °C it is at its best, and a human enzyme at 72 °C would be denatured.')],
 ('An enzyme gives these relative rates: 20 °C: 25, 30 °C: 50, 40 °C: 100, 50 °C: 30, 60 °C: 0. Estimate the optimum and explain the drop above it.',[
  '<b>Optimum.</b> The highest rate is at 40 °C (100).',
  '<b>Rise.</b> From 20 to 40 °C the rate doubles each 10 °C, as expected from more frequent and energetic collisions (Q10 ≈ 2).',
  '<b>Drop.</b> Above 40 °C the enzyme begins to denature: heat breaks the weak interactions that fold the active site, so fewer enzymes can bind substrate. By 60 °C most are denatured.']),
 ['Saying the enzyme "dies". Enzymes are not alive; they denature.','Thinking the optimum is the same for all enzymes.','Saying peptide bonds break on mild heating.','Forgetting pH also changes enzyme shape.','Describing a high rate at a high temperature as an adaptation without noting the organism.'],
 ('Interpreting graphs',['Name the optimum from the peak, describe the rise (collisions, kinetic energy) and the fall (denaturation: shape lost, active site no longer fits), and link the optimum to the environment the enzyme works in.']),
 [('Pepsin works best at pH 2 because',['it is adapted to stomach acid','acid makes peptide bonds','it has no R groups','acid is its substrate'],0,'The enzyme\'s shape and charges are adapted to the acidic stomach.')])

mod('atp','ATP & coupled reactions',
 'Cells run on one common energy currency. Many reactions that would not go on their own are driven by splitting ATP.',
 ['<b>ATP</b> (adenosine triphosphate) has an adenine base, a ribose sugar and three phosphate groups. The phosphate groups are negatively charged and repel one another, so the bonds between them hold energy. Hydrolysis of the terminal phosphate (ATP + H₂O → ADP + P<sub>i</sub>) releases about 30.5 kJ/mol under standard conditions.',
  'Cells use this by <b>energy coupling</b>: an unfavorable (endergonic) reaction is linked to ATP hydrolysis, often by transferring the phosphate onto the reactant, so the combined ΔG is negative. ATP is then rebuilt from ADP + P<sub>i</sub> using energy from respiration or photosynthesis. A cell turns over its ATP supply constantly.'],
 ('energy was something that "gets released" and then is gone','that cells store energy in a rechargeable molecule','how an unfavorable reaction proceeds by being coupled to a favorable one','ATP hydrolysis, phosphorylation and coupling'),
 [('ATP + H₂O → ADP + P<sub>i</sub>','ΔG ≈ −30.5 kJ/mol'),('net ΔG = ΔG₁ + ΔG₂','coupled reactions'),('ΔG < 0: favorable (exergonic)','ΔG > 0: needs energy'),('ADP + P<sub>i</sub> + energy → ATP','from respiration or light')],
 [lab('ap-lab','Coupling a reaction to ATP',canvas='ap-canvas',aria='Energy bars: an endergonic reaction, ATP hydrolysis, and the net change.',
   ctl=[('ap-dg','Reaction needs',5,60,1,28)],
   out=[('ap-net','net ΔG with 1 ATP','x'),('ap-sp','spontaneous?','a'),('ap-n','ATP needed to drive it','v')],own=True),
  stagelab('ap-lab2','The ATP cycle',canvas='ap-canvas2',aria='ATP is hydrolysed to ADP and phosphate to power work, and rebuilt using energy from food or light.',p='ac',n=4)],
 [('Set the reaction to need +20 kJ/mol. Does one ATP make it go? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ap-dg",20]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Yes: −30.5 + 20 = −10.5 kJ/mol, which is negative, so the coupled reaction is favorable.'),
  ('Set +45 kJ/mol. How many ATP are needed? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ap-dg",45]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','One ATP gives net +14.5 (not favorable). Two give −16 kJ/mol: favorable. Two ATP are needed.')],
 ('The synthesis of glutamine from glutamate and ammonia has ΔG = +14 kJ/mol. When coupled to ATP hydrolysis (−30.5 kJ/mol), find the net ΔG and decide if it is favorable.',[
  '<b>Add.</b> +14 + (−30.5) = −16.5 kJ/mol.',
  '<b>Decide.</b> Net ΔG is negative, so the coupled process is favorable.',
  '<b>How.</b> The phosphate is transferred to glutamate first, making it reactive enough to combine with ammonia.']),
 ['Saying the energy is stored in the bonds as if breaking a bond releases energy. The release comes from forming more stable products.','Thinking ATP is a long-term energy store. It is short term; fats and glycogen are storage.','Forgetting that net ΔG must be negative for the coupled reaction to go.','Mixing up ADP and ATP.','Ignoring that ATP must be re-made.'],
 ('Writing the explanation',['A good answer states the reaction is endergonic, that ATP hydrolysis is exergonic, that the two are coupled (often by phosphorylation) so net ΔG is negative, and that cellular respiration or photosynthesis regenerates ATP.']),
 [('Coupling an endergonic reaction (ΔG = +12 kJ/mol) to ATP hydrolysis (ΔG = −30.5 kJ/mol) gives a net ΔG of',['−18.5 kJ/mol','+42.5 kJ/mol','−42.5 kJ/mol','+18.5 kJ/mol'],0,'12 + (−30.5) = −18.5 kJ/mol.')])

mod('photo','Photosynthesis',
 'Plants make sugar from air and water, and the energy for it comes from light. The process has two linked stages: one that captures light energy and one that spends it to build sugar.',
 ['In the <b>light reactions</b> (thylakoid membranes), light excites electrons in photosystems. Water is split to replace them, releasing O₂. The electrons pass along an electron transport chain, pumping H⁺ into the thylakoid space. The H⁺ gradient drives <b>ATP synthase</b> (chemiosmosis) to make ATP, and the electrons end up in NADPH.',
  'In the <b>Calvin cycle</b> (stroma), the enzyme <b>rubisco</b> attaches CO₂ to RuBP (carbon fixation). ATP and NADPH from the light reactions reduce the product to G3P, a three-carbon sugar. Some G3P leaves to make glucose and the rest regenerates RuBP. Overall: 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂. The oxygen comes from the water.'],
 ('plants "eat" soil or just grow from sunlight','that light energy becomes chemical energy in two linked stages','where the oxygen and the carbon in sugar come from','light reactions, Calvin cycle, and limiting factors'),
 [('6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂','overall'),('light reactions: H₂O → O₂, make ATP and NADPH','thylakoid membrane'),('Calvin cycle: CO₂ → G3P using ATP and NADPH','stroma'),('3 CO₂ → 1 G3P: 9 ATP and 6 NADPH','per G3P')],
 [stagelab('ph-lab','Photosynthesis step by step',canvas='ph-canvas',aria='A chloroplast section showing the thylakoid membrane and stroma. Each stage highlights where the light reactions and Calvin cycle events occur.',p='ph',n=6),
  lab('ph-lab2','What limits photosynthesis?',canvas='ph-canvas2',aria='Rate of photosynthesis against light intensity for the chosen carbon dioxide level and temperature, with a marker at the current light.',
   ctl=[('ph-l','Light intensity',0,100,1,30),('ph-c','CO₂ level',0,100,1,60),('ph-t','Temperature',5,50,1,25)],
   out=[('ph-r','rate (relative)','x'),('ph-lim','limiting factor','a')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the stage where the H⁺ gradient is used. What is made? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ph-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','ATP: H⁺ flows through ATP synthase from the thylakoid space into the stroma, driving phosphorylation of ADP.'),
  ('Set light to 90 and CO₂ to 10. What limits the rate? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ph-l",90],["#ph-c",10]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','CO₂: the Calvin cycle is short of substrate, so more light does not help.'),
  ('Set temperature to 45 °C with plenty of light and CO₂. Why does the rate drop? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ph-l",90],["#ph-c",90],["#ph-t",45]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','High temperature denatures enzymes (like rubisco), so the rate falls even though light and CO₂ are plentiful.')],
 ('How many ATP and NADPH are needed for the Calvin cycle to make one molecule of glucose (6 CO₂ fixed)?',[
  '<b>Per 3 CO₂.</b> Making one G3P takes 9 ATP and 6 NADPH.',
  '<b>Glucose needs 6 CO₂.</b> That is two rounds of 3 CO₂, making 2 G3P.',
  '<b>Totals.</b> 2 × 9 = 18 ATP and 2 × 6 = 12 NADPH.']),
 ['Saying the oxygen released comes from CO₂. It comes from water.','Saying the Calvin cycle needs no light at all. It depends on ATP and NADPH made in the light reactions.','Putting the light reactions in the stroma and the Calvin cycle in the thylakoid.','Mixing up photosynthesis and respiration electron carriers (NADPH vs NADH).','Forgetting that the light reactions also need a source of electrons: water.'],
 ('Experiment questions',['Light-colour or intensity experiments measure O₂ production, CO₂ uptake or the floating of leaf discs. Predict the trend, name the limiting factor at each region of the graph, and explain it using the stage affected (light reactions for light; Calvin cycle for CO₂ and temperature-sensitive enzymes).']),
 [('The oxygen released in photosynthesis comes from',['water','carbon dioxide','glucose','ATP'],0,'Water is split in photosystem II, releasing O₂.')])

mod('resp','Cellular respiration',
 'Cells get usable energy by taking sugar apart a little at a time and capturing the energy in ATP.',
 ['Overall: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + energy (ATP and heat). It happens in four steps. <b>Glycolysis</b> (cytoplasm) splits glucose into 2 pyruvate with a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH. <b>Pyruvate oxidation</b> (mitochondrial matrix) converts each pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, releasing CO₂ and making NADH. The <b>citric acid cycle</b> (matrix) completes the breakdown, releasing CO₂ and making ATP, NADH and FADH₂. The <b>electron transport chain</b> (inner membrane) takes electrons from NADH and FADH₂, pumps H⁺ across the membrane and uses O₂ as the final electron acceptor (forming water).',
  'The resulting H⁺ gradient drives <b>ATP synthase</b> (chemiosmosis), which makes most of the ATP: roughly 30–32 per glucose in all. Without oxygen the chain stops, NADH cannot be recycled, and the cell must use fermentation instead.'],
 ('energy came out of food all at once','that energy is released in many small steps and captured by carriers','why oxygen is needed and where the CO₂ comes from','glycolysis, citric acid cycle, electron transport and chemiosmosis'),
 [('glycolysis: 1 glucose → 2 pyruvate, net 2 ATP, 2 NADH','cytoplasm'),('per glucose: 6 CO₂ released','from pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle'),('about 30–32 ATP per glucose (aerobic)','mostly from ATP synthase'),('O₂ is the final electron acceptor → H₂O','electron transport chain')],
 [stagelab('rs-lab','Respiration step by step',canvas='rs-canvas',aria='Mitochondrion and cytoplasm with the four stages of respiration highlighted in turn.',p='rs',n=5),
  lab('rs-lab2','Tally for glucose molecules',canvas='rs-canvas2',aria='Bars showing ATP from each stage of aerobic respiration for the chosen number of glucose molecules.',
   ctl=[('rs-g','Glucose molecules',1,5,1,1)],
   out=[('rs-atp','ATP (about)','x'),('rs-co2','CO₂ released','a'),('rs-o2','O₂ used','v'),('rs-h2o','H₂O made','')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the electron transport stage. What is the role of O₂? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#rs-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','It is the final electron acceptor, combining with electrons and H⁺ to form water. Without it the chain backs up.'),
  ('Set 3 glucose molecules. How much CO₂ is released and how much O₂ is used? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#rs-g",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','18 CO₂ and 18 O₂: six of each per glucose.')],
 ('Starting from 1 glucose, how many CO₂ are made in glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle, and where does the electron transport chain get its electrons?',[
  '<b>Glycolysis.</b> No CO₂ (glucose is split into two 3-carbon pyruvates).',
  '<b>Pyruvate oxidation.</b> 1 CO₂ per pyruvate, so 2.',
  '<b>Citric acid cycle.</b> 2 CO₂ per turn, 2 turns per glucose: 4. Total 6, matching the 6 carbons of glucose.',
  '<b>Electrons.</b> From NADH and FADH₂ made in the earlier steps.']),
 ['Thinking CO₂ is released in the electron transport chain. It comes from pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle.','Putting glycolysis in the mitochondrion.','Saying oxygen is used up in glycolysis.','Confusing the H⁺ gradient (made by the chain) with ATP (made by ATP synthase).','Memorizing 36 ATP; the commonly accepted figure is about 30–32.'],
 ('Predict and explain',['If a poison blocks the electron transport chain, NADH and FADH₂ accumulate, the H⁺ gradient collapses, ATP production falls sharply, and the cell can only run glycolysis. Questions often ask what happens to O₂ use, ATP and NADH levels: reason along the pathway.']),
 [('Where is most of the ATP in aerobic respiration made?',['by ATP synthase using the H⁺ gradient','in glycolysis','in the citric acid cycle','in the cytoplasm'],0,'Chemiosmosis in the inner mitochondrial membrane makes most ATP.')])

mod('ferment','Fermentation & anaerobic respiration',
 'Without oxygen the electron transport chain stops, but glycolysis can keep running if the cell can recycle its NADH.',
 ['Glycolysis needs NAD⁺ to keep going. When oxygen is absent, the chain cannot re-oxidise NADH to NAD⁺. <b>Fermentation</b> solves this by using pyruvate (or a product of it) as the electron acceptor so NAD⁺ is regenerated. In yeast, pyruvate becomes <b>ethanol</b> and CO₂ (<b>alcoholic fermentation</b>). In animal muscle, pyruvate becomes <b>lactate</b> (<b>lactic acid fermentation</b>).',
  'Fermentation makes only the <b>2 ATP</b> of glycolysis per glucose, compared with about 30–32 with oxygen. So a fermenting cell must use about fifteen times as much glucose for the same ATP. Some prokaryotes carry out <b>anaerobic respiration</b> with an acceptor other than O₂ at the end of an electron transport chain.'],
 ('cells simply stopped when oxygen ran out','that cells can keep making a little ATP by recycling NADH','why yeast makes bread rise and muscles burn','fermentation pathways and their ATP yield'),
 [('yeast: pyruvate → ethanol + CO₂','NAD⁺ regenerated'),('muscle: pyruvate → lactate','NAD⁺ regenerated'),('fermentation: 2 ATP per glucose','from glycolysis only'),('aerobic: about 30–32 ATP per glucose','electron transport chain')],
 [lab('fe-lab','Oxygen or not?',canvas='fe-canvas',aria='Flow diagram: glucose to pyruvate, then to aerobic respiration or to fermentation depending on oxygen, with ATP counts.',
   seg=('fe-org','Cell',[('yeast','yeast'),('muscle','muscle cell')]),
   ctl=[('fe-o2','Oxygen available',0,100,1,0),('fe-need','ATP the cell needs',30,120,5,60)],
   out=[('fe-path','pathway','x'),('fe-prod','products','a'),('fe-atp','ATP per glucose','v'),('fe-glu','glucose needed for that ATP','')],own=True)],
 [('Yeast with no oxygen and 60 ATP needed. How much glucose? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#fe-org [data-k=yeast]"],["#fe-o2",0],["#fe-need",60]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','60 ATP ÷ 2 ATP per glucose = 30 glucose.'),
  ('Now add oxygen (100). How does the glucose needed change? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#fe-o2",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','About 60/30 = 2 glucose: respiration with oxygen is roughly fifteen times as efficient.')],
 ('A muscle cell needs 90 ATP. Compare the glucose needed during hard exercise (lactic fermentation, 2 ATP per glucose) with rest (aerobic, about 30 ATP per glucose).',[
  '<b>Fermentation.</b> 90 / 2 = 45 glucose.',
  '<b>Aerobic.</b> 90 / 30 = 3 glucose.',
  '<b>Conclusion.</b> Fermentation uses 15 times as much glucose, and the lactate (and acidity) it builds up contributes to fatigue.']),
 ['Saying fermentation makes no ATP. It makes 2 per glucose via glycolysis.','Saying fermentation produces NADH. It consumes it, regenerating NAD⁺.','Thinking muscles make ethanol or yeast makes lactate.','Mixing fermentation (no electron transport chain) with anaerobic respiration (other final acceptor).','Forgetting that pyruvate is the starting point of both aerobic and anaerobic pathways.'],
 ('Why it matters',['Fermentation lets cells survive without oxygen for a while. Its purpose is to regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis continues; the ATP is what keeps the cell alive. If asked why the cell does not just stop, answer in terms of NAD⁺.']),
 [('The main purpose of fermentation is to',['regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue','make large amounts of ATP','produce oxygen','break down ATP'],0,'Recycling NAD⁺ allows glycolysis to keep making its 2 ATP.')])
